# reproductions.agg
# ======================================================================
#
# Every DecL program in *Reproductions*, the book of worked examples that
# rebuilds published actuarial and numerical results with `aggregate`.
# Each declaration carries a note{} naming the paper it comes from and
# saying in one line what it is.
#
# See https://blog.mynl.com/posts/notes/2026-10-08-Aggregate-Reproductions/
#
# Generated from the book by `make_agg.py`; do not edit by hand. Programs
# are in canonical form, which is what the parser understood rather than
# what was typed, and are named `<CitationKey>.<declared name>` so that
# nothing in the file shadows anything else. Declarations sharing a shape
# are sampled two apiece, as the book appendix samples them, and a model
# built by more than one chapter is listed once. The shipped severity
# curves are exempt from the sampling, each being its own curve rather
# than an arithmetic variant of the one above it.
#
# 140 programs from 28 chapters.
#
# Read it back with `build.load("reproductions.agg")`, or parse it
# without loading with `build.interpret_file("reproductions.agg")`.
# Sections below are the book chapters that built the programs; the
# name and the note give the paper.
#
# Every declaration here parses. Not all of them build on a default
# grid: where a severity has infinite variance or no mean, the bucket
# sizer has nothing to size from and raises rather than guessing, which
# is the behavior you want. Give those a limit or an explicit hints{}
# clause. The severity curves chapter of the book says which and why.


